# The conformance corpus is BYTES, not text, and git must not touch it.
#
# These files are the exact input a reader is handed. One case carries CRLF on
# purpose, because serial consoles emit CRLF and a reader that only accepts LF
# fails on real hardware while passing every test written on a workstation.
#
# WHY THIS IS NEEDED. A line-ending normalizer that rewrites tracked text files
# to LF would silently empty that case: the file would still be there, the test
# would still run, and it would still pass -- while testing nothing it was
# written to test. A fixture a tool is allowed to "fix" stops being a fixture.
#
# `-text` means no end-of-line conversion in either direction, on any platform,
# for any checkout.
#
# VERIFY IT RATHER THAN TRUSTING IT, and not with grep: `grep $'\r'` reported
# no carriage returns in a file that holds eight of them, which nearly got this
# case declared dead while it was working correctly. Count the bytes:
#
#   python -c "print(open('tests/corpus/crlf.report','rb').read().count(b'\r'))"
#
# Eight is correct. Zero means the protection below stopped working, and the
# `crlf` case is now green for the wrong reason.
tests/corpus/** -text

# The expectations are read line-by-line by eleven runners in eleven languages.
# Pinning them to LF keeps every runner seeing the same bytes rather than
# whatever the checkout decided.
tests/corpus/*.expect -text eol=lf
tests/corpus/INDEX    -text eol=lf
